  "comments": "Australia&#039;s first 12-sided 50-cent coin issued into circulation by the Royal Australian Mint (RAM) in 1969 replacing the 80% silver round 50-cent of 1966.<br />\r\nNo 50 cent coins were issued in 1967 and 1968.<br /><br />\r\n<span style=\"font-style:italic;\">* Diameter note:</span> 12-sided (dodecagonal) shape - 31.5mm across flats, 32.5mm point to point.<br /><br />\r\nFor the variety &quot;two parallel horizontal bars behind Emu&#039;s head&quot; for the years 1979 and 1980, this is what you have to look for:<br /><br />\r\n<a href=\"https://en.numista.com/catalogue/images/61fd2500a8633.jpg\"><img src=\"https://en.numista.com/catalogue/images/miniatures/61fd2500a8633.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"200\" height=\"150\" /></a><br /><br />\r\nOfficially in 1977 only Queen Elizabeth II Silver Jubilee 50 cent coins were struck by the RAM. However, there are 7 known examples of a COA (Coat of Arms) 1977 50 cent coin. These were either minted accidentally or deliberately (Mint Sport). None have been seen for a while and would fetch a very good price in the current market.<br /><br />\r\nSeven Mules are known to exist made from the 1977 obverse and a standard Coat of Arms Reverse.<br />\r\n<a href=\"https://en.numista.com/catalogue/images/603060c9702aa.jpg\"><img src=\"https://en.numista.com/catalogue/images/miniatures/603060c9702aa.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"200\" height=\"150\" /></a>",
